[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[manpage_begin plotanim n 0.2]
[see_also Plotchart]
[keywords animation plotting]
[moddesc   Plotchart]
[titledesc {Simple animations in Plotchart plots}]
[require Tcl [opt 8.5]]
[require Tk [opt 8.5]]
[require plotanim [opt 0.2]]
[description]

The [package plotanim] package draws simple objects in a
[package Plotchart] plot and moves them, e.g. to show the motion of
points in a mathematical animation.  It is based on Arjen Markus' Tcl
wiki page "Creating Mathematical Animations".

[para]

The objects are given in the world coordinates of the plot, so the
canvas must hold a plot whose scaling is set, e.g. one created with
[cmd ::Plotchart::createXYPlot].  Each object has a name, unique for
the canvas.  The kinds of objects are:

[list_begin definitions]
[def [const point]] An invisible point, used for example as the center
of a rotation or as an end point of lines.
[def [const dot]] A small dot.
[def [const circle]] A circle with a center and a radius.  The radius
is given in units of the x-axis.
[def [const line]] A line between two points.
[def [const rectangle]] A rectangle given by two opposite corners.
[list_end]

All commands are in the namespace [namespace ::Plotchart::anim] and
take the canvas [arg w] as their first argument.  Wherever a
[arg point] is expected, it can be given either as a list of the x and
y coordinates or as the name of an existing object, whose first
coordinates are then used.

[section {COMMANDS}]

[subsection {Describing objects}]

These commands return a description of an object, to be passed to
[cmd create] or [cmd update].  The [arg option]s are canvas item
options (e.g. [option -fill], [option -width]); they are used only by
[cmd create].

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::point] [arg w] [arg point] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]
[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::dot] [arg w] [arg point] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]

Describe an invisible point or a dot at [arg point].

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::circle] [arg w] [arg center] [arg radius] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]

Describe a circle.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::line] [arg w] [arg point1] [arg point2] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]
[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::rectangle] [arg w] [arg point1] [arg point2] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]

Describe a line or a rectangle.

[list_end]

[subsection {Creating and changing objects}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::create] [arg w] [arg name] [arg description]]

Creates the object [arg name] from the [arg description] returned by
one of the commands above, and draws it.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::update] [arg w] [arg name] [arg "description element ..."]]

Moves the existing object [arg name] to the position given by a new
description of the same kind, given as separate arguments, e.g.
[example {::Plotchart::anim::update $w p {*}[::Plotchart::anim::point $w {1 2}]}]
An error is raised if the description is of another kind.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::coords] [arg w] [arg name]]

Returns the coordinates of the object [arg name]: [arg "x y"] for a
point, dot or circle (its center), and [arg "x1 y1 x2 y2"] for a line
or rectangle.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::id] [arg w] [arg name]]

Returns the canvas item of the object [arg name] (not for points), to
change it with ordinary canvas commands.

[list_end]

[subsection {Moving objects}]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::translate] [arg w] [arg names] [arg vector]]

Moves the objects in the list [arg names] by [arg vector], a list of
the x and y distances (or the name of an object, whose coordinates are
used as the distances).

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::rotate] [arg w] [arg names] [arg center] [arg angle]]

Rotates the objects in the list [arg names] about the point
[arg center] by [arg angle], in radians, counterclockwise.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::morph] [arg w] [arg names] [arg shift]]

Changes the shape of the lines and rectangles in the list
[arg names]: [arg shift] is a list [arg "dx1 dy1 dx2 dy2"] of the
distances by which their two points are moved.  An error is raised for
points and dots.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::add] [arg w] [arg point] [arg vector]]

Returns the coordinates of [arg point] moved by [arg vector], without
changing anything.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::track] [arg w] [method start] [arg name] [opt [arg "option value ..."]]]
[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::track] [arg w] [method next] [arg name]]
[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::track] [arg w] [method stop] [arg name]]

Draws the path of the object [arg name].  [method start] begins a new
line at its current position, with the given canvas line options,
[method next] extends the line to its current position, and
[method stop] ends the tracking; the line drawn stays on the canvas.

[list_end]

[subsection Timing]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::pause] [arg w] [arg ms]]

Waits [arg ms] milliseconds while processing events, so that the
display is updated and the application stays responsive, then
returns.  Use it between the steps of an animation.

[call [cmd ::Plotchart::anim::animate] [arg w] [opt "[option -duration] [arg ms]"] [opt "[option -pause] [arg ms]"] [option -body] [arg body]]

Changes the shape of lines and rectangles gradually, in steps
[option -pause] milliseconds apart (default 20), over
[option -duration] milliseconds (default 1000), and returns when it
is done.  [arg body] is a list of the form
[example {morphto name point1 point2 ?morphto name point1 point2 ...?}]
which moves the two points of the line or rectangle [arg name] to
[arg point1] and [arg point2].

[list_end]

[section EXAMPLE]

A dot moving on a circle, leaving a trace:

[example {
package require Plotchart
package require plotanim

pack [canvas .c -width 400 -height 400]
set p [::Plotchart::createXYPlot .c {-2 2 1} {-2 2 1}]

set A ::Plotchart::anim
${A}::create .c centre [${A}::point .c {0 0}]
${A}::create .c moon   [${A}::dot .c {1 0} -fill red]
${A}::track .c start moon -fill red

for {set i 0} {$i < 100} {incr i} {
    ${A}::rotate .c moon centre [expr {3.14159 / 50}]
    ${A}::track .c next moon
    ${A}::pause .c 20
}
}]

